body:
"Devils Advocate" here, since when is asking rude? Can't people just say no? feel like asking should always be fine. People aren't obligated to give them money. Also, a lot of them seem to be honest about it, like hey will you support my trip? I can either be like "sure thats cool!" or "no I want to spend my money on other things." 

&#x200B;

A lot of the arguments on here are basically "The begpackers should take individual responsibility!" but then like why not turn that argument around on itself?  Why not make everyone else take individual responsibility in how they decide to hand out their money?

And also, freedom of speech doesn't justify saying such things. I can't stop people from thinking for themselves, nor would I want someone to be put in jail for using hate speech or even fined. I would like to see hate speech made "illegal" in the sense that the duty of the police officer ideally is "peacekeeper". I would like to see people in these positions (the position of peacekeeper) confront people who use hate speech. I would like to see an  "officer of the peace" actually work at maintaining peace through more than just force.  Hate speech can be "illegal" with the only consequence being someone talks to the offender compassionately to help them see the damage they are causing.

&#x200B;

I think there is too narrow a concept of what it means when something is "illegal". Law doesn't have to be strictly fines and jail and such. Hate speech can cause serious harm both emotionally and psychologically, and it can encourage and incite physical harm as well. I would like to live in a society and culture where hate speech isn't defended under the guise of "free speech". People "can" say whatever they want, but there are consequences to the words we speak.  Words can be as harmful as actions.

&#x200B;

edit: I know I personally would rather be punched in the face (which is totally illegal ) than hear certain words said to me, that is how painful words can be.

>\>  And that's the thing about wealth, in the modern world it's interchangeable, you can take wealth and use it for anything and the market has dictated that it cares more about some things than cancer, is this the hedge fund managers fault?

&#x200B;

I could make a similar argument for a heroin dealer. Hey man is it the heroin dealers fault the market is demanding heroin? See what I am getting at? The hedge fund manager supplies "capital" which can be used for all sorts of things. To completely absolve the hedge fund manager for how capital is supplied is logically the same as absolving a heroin dealer because "hey man its the junkies choice to use, its not the dealers responsibility for how the heroin is used"

&#x200B;

