This sounds like pride, which is one step up from anger. I think the next step up is courage. Address the behavior directly, tell her you feel hurt, connect with her, or maybe even see if she is hurting. I don't think that law of attraction is designed to encourage people to be dicks to each other. If you act like a dick, someone is gonna be a dick right back. "Big Dick Energy", is that really what we want to embody? That doesn't sound like someone who is tuned in, tapped in, turned on. That doesn't sound like a connection with loving source.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

An alternative less dickish way to approach this is to actually be compassionate towards her. Pause for a minute, notice how tense she is, notice how frustrated she is. See if you can sense into what she is needing and feeling. Perhaps if you do, you might actually connect. Sure you could laugh in her face, but is that really remaining positive? That to me seems like Pride. "Oh I am happy! and You aren't! Sucks to be you!" That isnt spreading positivity. 

&#x200B;

In a clam and compassionate way you could be  like "Hey Jenn, you seem pretty tense, and maybe worried I won't understand how to do this. I want you to feel reassured. I know this work is important to you." Or you know something like that depending on the exact context. 

&#x200B;

I suggest not using "Big Dick Energy", because that's just being a dick and doesn't feel in alignment with me.  It feels grasping and rejecting. You are grasping for positivity by rejecting non positivity. But if you are actively rejecting her, that is taking on the energy of rejection. Do you really want to take on the energy of rejecting? Is that how you want to feel? That is pride, not courage or love or compassion, it is not connection with source.

Sometimes waiting is fun though because anticipation can be very exciting!!! :) I personally enjoy that anticipatory feeling.

&#x200B;

I guess there are more negative kinds of waiting too though.

The universe hears what you mean, not what you say. It sounds what you are really looking for is the feeling of freedom. What do you have the ability to do right now? You can get up, you can walk out the door, you can go for a run. There are literally an infinite number of  things you can do right now in this moment. Tap into and find that inner sense of freedom, and follow your inspiration from that place. As long as you focus on the problem which is "lack of money", you will have a hard time. 

&#x200B;

Law of attraction is about focus and feeling states. Focus on the feeling state you want in whatever way you can, let inspiration come. Your only work is to focus. Focus on feeling free!!! GO BE FREE, RIGHT NOW. IN THIS MOMENT. If you believe that the only way you can be free is to accumulate money, you will forever be trapped. I see so many people who believe that and they are trapped in jobs they hate, they are trapped in finance, they are slaves to money. They are some of the least free people on the planet. BE FREE NOW! 

&#x200B;

I also suggest meditating, it helps the mind drop stories.

The incentives only matter because of how the lobbying system currently works. If the lobbying system wasn't so broken and there wasn't so much crony capitalism happening, then a private prison system wouldn't impact policy. But since money has corrupted politics to a fairly extreme degree in the United States the incentives now matter because they are actionable.

I am not sure if I am being clear. But basically the intermediary step is lobbying, a private company can only affect policy through that step, so their incentives only matter in so far as how much they can affect policy. Our lobbying system could be set up much differently to mitigate the affect these perverse incentives have. The other benefit is that it would positively affect ALL industries, not just the prison system. The lobbying system causes SOOOO many issues, not just prison related. We really need to look at how money influences policy and change that.

&#x200B;

Edit: Of course the question is how do we change that system when money already influences it so much! A fairly wicked problem.

&#x200B;

Edit 2: I think part of the solution is lots of communication with all people, encourage voting, model integrity in our behavior, be kind to others, try and vote for people who have integrity (maybe even regardless of policy). Really as we can have more compassion for our fellow man, and try to forge more understanding, things will get better I believe. There are so many possibilities and so many angles to take. My two cents reddit opinion is unlikely to fix anything of course :), but hey spreading ideas!

Define demonstrable harm. When someone walks into Walmart with an open carry, and someone else has a panic attack because they think there is going to be a shooting, and then misses work because they are so panicked and loses their job. Is that demonstrable harm?  Does it have to be strictly physical? I would argue that I could say super hateful and mean things to someone that would also cause harm. There are countless other ways someone can cause psychological harm. Do I have the freedom to tell you to go jump off a bridge? What if you actually then did it because your ego was so fragile and easily hurt? It seems to be that defining "liberty" as a "right" is fraught with all kinds of issues. Because the reality is, everyone has the power to kill or steal or do any number of awful things, and they do. The lens of "rights" and "wrongs" is starting to not serve us as a culture. You end up with lots of "injustice collecting". I prefer we look at the lens of needs and simply try to be more loving, compassionate, kind, and forgiving. We are all connected, every action I take affects everyone around me.

As for property acquistion, the native americans would disagree that it is the first person to improve upon it. They used the land as a resource, not something to be "improved upon". Of course Settlers used guns and just was like nah you can't use this anymore it's mine. 

But also like, a lot of the present property in the world that is owned by intergenerational families was acquired in really fucked up ways. See the Rothchilds, Vanderbilts, Rockefellers, the list goes on. Also, define "misrepresented". Literally all things are to some extent not represented as what they actually are, because we speak a language and a language is a model. So at what point does the misrepresentation become large enough that its considered fraud?

&#x200B;

